from . import n
n("chemical_bonding","Chemical bonding","4",(1e-10,1e-9),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Atoms bond by sharing or transferring electrons to lower their total energy. The main types are ionic, covalent and metallic, with weaker hydrogen bonds and van der Waals forces between molecules.",
 "Electronegativity difference tells whether electrons are shared evenly (nonpolar), unevenly (polar) or transferred (ionic). All chemistry is electromagnetism acting through quantum mechanics.",
 "C–C 348 kJ/mol, C=C 614, C≡C 839, O–H 463; NaCl lattice energy ≈787 kJ/mol; Pauling χ(F)=3.98, χ(Na)=0.93.",
 "Bond models are classifications along a continuum.",
 em="quantum_orbitals coulomb_law periodic_table",ev="X-ray crystallography|Bond-energy tables|Spectroscopy",refs="CHE 7; CAF 4; ORG 1",ex="molecular_shapes intermolecular_forces thermochemistry",sim="molecule")
n("molecular_shapes","Molecular geometry and orbitals (VSEPR, MO theory)","4",(1e-10,1e-9),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Electron pairs repel each other, so molecules adopt shapes such as bent water or tetrahedral methane. Molecular orbital theory spreads electrons over whole molecules.",
 "Hybridisation (sp, sp², sp³) is a way to describe bond directions. Delocalised orbitals explain benzene's stability and the colour of dyes.",
 "Bond angles: CH₄ 109.5°, H₂O 104.5°, NH₃ 107°; bond order=(n_bonding−n_antibonding)/2.",
 "VSEPR is qualitative; heavy-element and transition-metal compounds often deviate.",
 em="chemical_bonding quantum_orbitals",ev="Infrared and microwave spectra|Crystal structures",refs="CHE 7; CHE 8; ORG 1",ex="organic_functional_groups",sim="molecule")
n("intermolecular_forces","Intermolecular forces and hydrogen bonding","4",(1e-10,1e-8),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Neutral molecules still attract each other through temporary and permanent charge imbalances. Hydrogen bonds are especially strong and shape water, proteins and DNA.",
 "These forces set boiling points, solubility and how proteins fold. London dispersion forces arise from fluctuating electron clouds.",
 "H-bond ≈ 5–30 kJ/mol (water ≈ 21); dispersion ∝ r⁻⁶; boiling points: CH₄ 112 K, H₂O 373 K.",
 "Pairwise-additive models are approximate.",
 em="chemical_bonding coulomb_law",ev="Boiling-point trends|Water structure",refs="CHE 10; CAF 5",ex="liquid_water_properties protein_structure dna_structure",sim="liquid:water")
n("liquid_water_properties","Why water is special (and why ice floats)","4",(1e-10,1e-3),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Water molecules form hydrogen bonds, which give it a high boiling point, large heat capacity and strong solvent power. Ice is less dense than liquid water because bonds lock molecules into an open lattice.",
 "Floating ice insulates lakes so life can survive winter. Water's maximum density near 4 °C and its high heat capacity moderate climate.",
 "ρ_ice=917 kg/m³, ρ_water(4 °C)=1000; c_p=4.18 J g⁻¹K⁻¹; L_fus=334 J/g, L_vap=2260 J/g; dielectric constant 80.",
 "Anomalies fade at high temperature and in confinement.",
 em="intermolecular_forces molecular_shapes",ev="Density curves|Neutron scattering of water",refs="CHE 10; BIO 2",ex="water_cycle oceans_circulation cell_biology_intro",sim="liquid:water crystal")
n("states_of_matter","States of matter and gases","4",(1e-10,1e3),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Matter exists as solid, liquid, gas and plasma depending on how strongly particles interact compared with their thermal energy.",
 "Solids have fixed positions, liquids flow while staying dense, gases fill containers, and plasmas are ionised gases. Phase diagrams map the conditions.",
 "Ideal gas PV=nRT; van der Waals (P+an²/V²)(V−nb)=nRT; water triple point 273.16 K, 611.7 Pa.",
 "Real behaviour near critical points departs from simple laws.",
 em="kinetic_theory intermolecular_forces",ev="Phase diagrams|Gas-law experiments",refs="CHE 9; CHE 10",sim="gas:air liquid:water plasma crystal")
n("gas_air_properties","Composition and properties of air","4",(1e-10,1e5),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Dry air is about 78 % nitrogen, 21 % oxygen and about 1 % argon, with traces of carbon dioxide and other gases. Its pressure falls by half every ~5.5 km of altitude.",
 "Water vapour varies up to a few percent. Oxygen is a by-product of photosynthesis and was almost absent before about 2.4 billion years ago.",
 "N₂ 78.08 %, O₂ 20.95 %, Ar 0.93 %; ρ_air=1.2 kg/m³ at 20 °C; scale height H=kT/mg≈8.5 km.",
 "Barometric law assumes isothermal atmosphere.",
 em="kinetic_theory states_of_matter",ev="Sampling studies",refs="CHE 9",sim="air gas:air")
n("chemistry_solutions","Solutions and colloids","4",(1e-10,1e-6),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Substances dissolve when attractions to the solvent compensate for breaking their own bonds. Water's polarity dissolves salts and sugars but not oils.",
 "Concentration is measured as molarity; dissolved particles change boiling, freezing and osmotic pressure. Colloids such as milk are suspensions of tiny particles.",
 "M=n/V; Raoult P=xP°; osmotic Π=MRT; ΔT_f=K_f m.",
 "Ideal solutions assumed; ionic solutions deviate.",
 em="intermolecular_forces states_of_matter",ev="Freezing-point depression|Osmometry",refs="CHE 11; CAF 11",ex="membrane_transport")
n("thermochemistry","Thermochemistry and enthalpy","4",(1e-10,1e3),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Chemical reactions absorb or release heat because bonds break and form. Enthalpy change measures the heat at constant pressure.",
 "Hess's law lets you add reactions to find unknown enthalpies. Burning fuels releases energy because the products have stronger bonds.",
 "ΔH°_rxn=ΣΔH_f°(products)−ΣΔH_f°(reactants); CH₄+2O₂→CO₂+2H₂O ΔH=−890 kJ/mol; glucose combustion −2803 kJ/mol.",
 "Standard-state values at 298 K.",
 em="first_law chemical_bonding",ev="Bomb calorimetry",refs="CHE 5; CAF 5",ex="chemistry_gibbs")
n("chemistry_gibbs","Gibbs free energy and spontaneity","4",(1e-10,1e3),"C1","Chemistry",
 "A process happens on its own if it lowers the free energy, which balances heat released against disorder gained. Free energy sets which reactions can run and how far.",
 "Cells couple unfavourable reactions to ATP hydrolysis to drive them. Entropy matters most at high temperature.",
 "ΔG=ΔH−TΔS; ΔG°=−RT ln K; ATP hydrolysis ΔG°′≈−30.5 kJ/mol; ΔG=−nFE.",
 "Says whether a reaction can occur, not how fast.",
 em="second_law thermochemistry statistical_mechanics",ev="Equilibrium constants|Metabolic energetics",refs="CHE 16; BIO 6",ex="chemistry_equilibrium electrochemistry atp_energy")
n("chemistry_equilibrium","Chemical equilibrium and acid-base","4",(1e-10,1e1),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Reactions run both ways until forward and reverse rates match. Acids donate protons and bases accept them, and pH measures acidity on a logarithmic scale.",
 "Le Chatelier's principle: a system at equilibrium shifts to counter an imposed change. Buffers resist pH change, which is vital for blood (pH 7.4).",
 "K=[C]^c[D]^d/([A]^a[B]^b); pH=−log[H⁺]; pH=pKa+log([A⁻]/[HA]); K_w=10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C.",
 "Concentrations should be activities for non-dilute solutions.",
 em="chemistry_gibbs statistical_mechanics",ev="Titration curves|Blood buffering",refs="CHE 13; CHE 14; CHE 15",ex="ocean_acidification")
n("reaction_kinetics","Reaction kinetics and catalysis","4",(1e-10,1e1),"C1","Chemistry",
 "How fast a reaction goes depends on collisions with enough energy to cross an activation barrier. Catalysts and enzymes lower that barrier without being used up.",
 "Rate rises sharply with temperature; a 10 °C rise often doubles it. Transition state theory links rates to energy landscapes.",
 "k=A e^{−E_a/RT}; rate=k[A]^m[B]^n; first-order t_{1/2}=ln2/k; Eyring k=(k_BT/h)e^{−ΔG‡/RT}.",
 "Rate laws must be measured; they are not read from stoichiometry.",
 em="statistical_mechanics chemical_bonding",ev="Stopped-flow kinetics|Femtochemistry",refs="CHE 12; CAF 12",ex="enzymes haber_bosch")
n("electrochemistry","Redox and electrochemistry","4",(1e-10,1e2),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Redox reactions move electrons from one species to another, and when the two halves are separated this produces an electric current. Batteries and corrosion are electrochemistry.",
 "Standard potentials rank how readily substances gain electrons. Cell voltage depends on concentrations via the Nernst equation.",
 "ΔG=−nFE; E=E°−(RT/nF)lnQ; F=96485 C/mol; Li-ion cell ≈3.6–3.7 V.",
 "Equilibrium potentials; kinetics (overpotential) matter in devices.",
 em="chemistry_gibbs",ev="Galvanic cells|Corrosion|Electroplating",refs="CHE 17; CAF 17",ex="batteries_tech photosynthesis_light oxidative_phosphorylation")
n("organic_functional_groups","Organic chemistry and functional groups","4",(1e-10,1e-8),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Carbon can form four bonds and chains, so it supports millions of compounds. Groups of atoms such as alcohols, acids and amines decide a molecule's reactions.",
 "Reaction mechanisms track electron pairs moving from nucleophile to electrophile. Chirality means a molecule and its mirror image can behave differently in living systems.",
 "sp³ C–C 154 pm, sp² C=C 134 pm, sp C≡C 120 pm; SN2 rate=k[RX][Nu]; benzene resonance ≈150 kJ/mol.",
 "Mechanisms are models consistent with kinetics and isotope tests.",
 em="molecular_shapes",ev="NMR, IR, MS identification|Total syntheses",refs="ORG 3; ORG 6; ORG 11; ORG 15",ex="biomolecules_overview polymers_chem pharmacology",sim="molecule")
n("stereochemistry_chirality","Stereochemistry and chirality","4",(1e-10,1e-8),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Many molecules exist as mirror images that cannot be superimposed. Biology uses almost only one hand: L-amino acids and D-sugars.",
 "The two forms of a drug can have different effects. Why life chose one handedness is unresolved.",
 "R/S via Cahn-Ingold-Prelog; specific rotation [α]=α/(l·c); enantiomeric excess ee=|R−S|/(R+S).",
 "Concept applies to any molecule with a stereogenic centre.",
 em="organic_functional_groups molecular_shapes",ev="Polarimetry|Thalidomide history",refs="ORG 5",ex="biomolecules_overview",fr="Origin of biological homochirality~open",fref="bennu_samples")
n("spectroscopy_methods","Spectroscopy: how we identify molecules","4",(1e-10,1e-2),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Molecules absorb specific energies of radio, infrared and visible light. NMR, IR and mass spectrometry reveal their structure and composition.",
 "NMR uses nuclear spin in a magnetic field, the same physics as MRI. Mass spectrometry weighs ions.",
 "NMR ν=γB/2π (¹H: 42.58 MHz/T); IR C=O stretch ≈1715 cm⁻¹; m/z=mass/charge.",
 "Needs reference data and sample prep.",
 em="quantum_orbitals electromagnetic_waves",ev="Standard laboratory practice",refs="ORG 12; ORG 13; CHE 6",ex="medical_imaging")
n("polymers_chem","Polymers and plastics","4",(1e-9,1e-3),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Polymers are long chains made of repeating units. Natural examples are DNA, proteins and cellulose; synthetic ones include polyethylene and nylon.",
 "Chain length and cross-linking determine stiffness and melting. Plastics are durable because their backbone bonds are strong and microbes lack enzymes for them.",
 "M_n, M_w, PDI=M_w/M_n; polyethylene (–CH₂–)ₙ T_m≈135 °C (HDPE); Tg of PMMA ≈105 °C.",
 "Entangled-chain dynamics described by reptation theory.",
 em="organic_functional_groups",ev="Industrial polymer science",refs="ORG 31",sim="molecule")
n("inorganic_coordination","Transition metals and coordination chemistry","4",(1e-10,1e-8),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Metals such as iron and copper bind molecules called ligands to form coloured, reactive complexes. They sit at the heart of haemoglobin, chlorophyll and many catalysts.",
 "Crystal field theory explains colour from d-electron splitting. Metalloproteins use this to carry oxygen and transfer electrons.",
 "Δ_oct splitting: [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ ≈20 300 cm⁻¹ (blue-green absorption); 18-electron rule.",
 "Models are semi-quantitative; DFT used for accuracy.",
 em="chemical_bonding periodic_table",ev="Crystal structures|UV-vis spectra",refs="CHE 19; CAF 19",ex="heme_oxygen")
n("haber_bosch","Nitrogen fixation and Haber-Bosch","4",(1e-10,1e7),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Plants need fixed nitrogen, which is scarce because N₂ has a very strong triple bond. The Haber-Bosch process makes ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen and feeds roughly half of humanity.",
 "It runs at ~400–500 °C and 150–300 atm over an iron catalyst. Industrial fixation now rivals natural land fixation.",
 "N₂+3H₂⇌2NH₃, ΔH=−92 kJ/mol; N≡N 945 kJ/mol; global NH₃ output ≈ 1.5×10⁸ t/yr.",
 "Energy-intensive; catalysts working at mild conditions are an active research target.",
 em="reaction_kinetics chemical_bonding",ev="Fertiliser statistics|Isotope tracers",refs="CHE 13; BIO 46",ex="nitrogen_cycle",fr="Mild-condition nitrogen fixation catalysts~open")
n("computational_chemistry","Computational chemistry (DFT, molecular dynamics)","4",(1e-10,1e-6),"C2","Chemistry",
 "Computers approximate quantum mechanics to predict molecule shapes and reactions. Density functional theory handles hundreds of atoms; molecular dynamics follows thousands to millions with simpler forces.",
 "Accuracy is good for ground-state organic chemistry but limited for strongly correlated systems like some transition-metal catalysts.",
 "E[ρ]=T_s+E_H+E_xc+∫Vρ; MD: m r̈=−∇U with Δt≈1–2 fs; chemical accuracy ≈1 kcal/mol (4.2 kJ/mol).",
 "Functional choice and sampling limits cap accuracy.",
 em="schrodinger quantum_orbitals",ev="Benchmark sets vs experiment",refs="CHE 8",fr="Strongly correlated electrons remain hard~open",fref="alphafold_limits")
# ---------- materials / condensed matter ----------
n("crystal_lattices","Crystals and lattices","5",(1e-10,1e-3),"C1","Materials",
 "In crystals atoms sit in repeating three-dimensional patterns. There are 14 Bravais lattices and 230 space groups.",
 "Crystal structure controls hardness, cleavage, optical and electronic properties. X-ray diffraction reveals the arrangement.",
 "Bragg: 2d sinθ=nλ; fcc packing fraction 0.74; Si lattice a=0.543 nm; NaCl a=0.564 nm.",
 "Real crystals contain defects.",
 em="chemical_bonding group_symmetry",ev="X-ray diffraction (Laue, Bragg)",refs="CHE 10; UP3 9",ex="band_theory minerals_rocks dislocations_strength",sim="crystal")
n("band_theory","Band theory of solids","5",(1e-10,1e-3),"C1","Condensed matter",
 "In solids, electron energy levels merge into bands with gaps between them. Whether the top band is full or has a gap decides if a material is a metal, insulator or semiconductor.",
 "Metals conduct because electrons can easily move to nearby levels. Semiconductors have gaps of about 1 eV that can be crossed by heat or light and adjusted by doping.",
 "E(k) bands; E_g(Si)=1.12 eV, E_g(GaAs)=1.42 eV, diamond 5.5 eV; Fermi level; σ=neμ.",
 "Independent-electron picture; strong correlations break it (Mott insulators).",
 em="schrodinger spin_pauli crystal_lattices",ev="Photoemission spectroscopy|Conductivity vs temperature",refs="UP3 9",ex="semiconductors_pn circuits_ohm superconductivity solar_cells")
n("semiconductors_pn","Semiconductors, p-n junctions and transistors","5",(1e-9,1e-2),"C1","Condensed matter",
 "Adding small amounts of impurities makes a semiconductor carry current with electrons (n-type) or holes (p-type). Joining them makes a diode, and three layers make a transistor.",
 "A transistor is a switch with a gate that controls the current. Billions of them form a processor.",
 "Shockley diode I=I_s(e^{V/V_T}−1), V_T=kT/q=25.9 mV; MOSFET threshold V_th≈0.3–1 V; modern gate pitch ~tens of nm.",
 "Quantum tunnelling and heat limit further miniaturisation.",
 em="band_theory",ev="Integrated circuits (1958 onward)",refs="UP3 9",ex="electronics_logic solar_cells led_lasers")
n("superconductivity","Superconductivity","5",(1e-10,1e-2),"C2","Condensed matter",
 "Below a critical temperature some materials conduct electricity with zero resistance and expel magnetic fields. Electrons pair up and move together.",
 "In conventional superconductors the pairing is mediated by lattice vibrations (BCS theory). High-temperature copper-oxide superconductors work above liquid-nitrogen temperature but their mechanism is not settled.",
 "BCS gap Δ≈1.76 k_BT_c; Nb T_c=9.2 K, YBa₂Cu₃O₇ T_c≈92 K; London depth λ_L=√(m/μ₀ne²).",
 "Room-temperature ambient-pressure superconductivity has not been verified; the 2023 LK-99 claim failed replication.",
 em="band_theory symmetry_breaking",ev="Meissner effect|MRI magnets|Josephson effect",refs="UP3 9",fr="Mechanism of cuprate superconductivity~open|LK-99 (2023) not reproduced~contradicted",ex="mri_tech")
n("magnetic_materials","Magnetic materials","5",(1e-10,1e-3),"C1","Condensed matter",
 "Iron, nickel and cobalt are ferromagnetic because electron spins align over regions called domains. Heating above the Curie temperature destroys the order.",
 "Hard drives store bits in magnetic domains. Magnetic order is a classic example of spontaneous symmetry breaking.",
 "Curie T_c(Fe)=1043 K; μ_B=9.274×10⁻²⁴ J/T; Curie-Weiss χ=C/(T−T_c).",
 "Exchange interaction is quantum mechanical in origin.",
 em="spin_pauli symmetry_breaking",ev="Hysteresis loops|Neutron scattering",refs="UP3 9",ex="geodynamo")
n("dislocations_strength","Defects, dislocations and material strength","5",(1e-9,1e-2),"C1","Materials",
 "Real metals are far weaker than perfect crystals because lines of misplaced atoms (dislocations) slide easily. Making them harder to move strengthens the metal.",
 "Alloying, work hardening and fine grains block dislocations. Steel's strength is tuned by carbon content and heat treatment.",
 "τ_theory≈G/10 but τ_real≈G/10³–10⁴; Hall-Petch σ_y=σ₀+k d^{−1/2}; steel yield ≈250–1500 MPa.",
 "Brittle ceramics fail by cracks instead (Griffith criterion).",
 em="crystal_lattices elasticity_materials",ev="Electron microscopy of dislocations",refs="UP1 12",ex="engineering_materials")
n("engineering_materials","Metals, ceramics, composites and glass","5",(1e-9,1e2),"C1","Materials",
 "Engineers choose materials by linking structure to properties: metals are tough and conductive, ceramics hard and heat-resistant, polymers light, composites combine strengths, and glass is a frozen liquid.",
 "Carbon-fibre composites reach steel's strength at a quarter of the weight. Glass has no long-range order, so it lacks sharp melting.",
 "Density: Al 2.70, steel 7.85, CFRP ~1.6 g/cm³; E: diamond 1050 GPa, glass 70 GPa.",
 "Properties depend on processing as well as composition.",
 em="dislocations_strength polymers_chem",ev="Materials databases",refs="ADM 1",ex="additive_manufacturing_tech",sim="rock")
n("soft_matter","Soft matter and liquid crystals","5",(1e-9,1e-3),"C2","Condensed matter",
 "Gels, foams, colloids, polymers and liquid crystals are soft because thermal motion is comparable to their binding energy. Cells are largely soft matter.",
 "Liquid crystals let screens switch light with small voltages. Self-assembly of lipids into bilayers is a soft-matter process.",
 "Entropic spring F=3k_BT x/(N b²); persistence length DNA ≈50 nm; Brownian D=k_BT/6πηr.",
 "No single unifying theory.",
 em="statistical_mechanics intermolecular_forces",ev="Colloid experiments",refs="UP2 2",ex="membrane_structure")
n("topological_matter","Topological phases and 2D materials","5",(1e-10,1e-4),"C2","Condensed matter",
 "Some materials have conduction that is protected by the global shape of their electron states. Graphene, one atom-thick carbon, and quantum-Hall systems are well-known examples.",
 "Quantum-Hall resistance is quantised to extreme precision, now used in electrical standards. Twisted layers (moiré materials) show correlated and superconducting phases.",
 "R_H=h/(νe²), h/e²=25 812.807 Ω; graphene mobility >10⁵ cm²/V s.",
 "Active research; practical devices still emerging.",
 em="band_theory symmetry_breaking",ev="Integer quantum Hall effect 1980|Graphene isolation 2004",refs="UP3 9",fr="Strange metals and moiré physics poorly understood~open")
n("solar_cells","Photovoltaics (solar cells)","7",(1e-9,1),"C1","Technology",
 "A solar cell is a p-n junction that turns absorbed photons into electric current. Silicon cells convert roughly 20–25 % of sunlight.",
 "The Shockley-Queisser limit caps a single-junction cell at about 33 %. Perovskites and tandem cells are research frontiers, with stability a main hurdle.",
 "SQ limit ≈33 % at E_g≈1.34 eV; Si record ≈26–27 %; P_sun at Earth 1361 W/m² (1000 W/m² at surface).",
 "Multijunction cells exceed single-junction limit.",
 em="semiconductors_pn",ev="Commercial panels",refs="UP3 9",fr="Perovskite cell stability~open")
